  <category>
    <pattern>STATE THE VARIATIONAL PRINCIPLE FOR QUANTUM MECHANICS AND EXPLAIN HOW IT PROVIDES AN UPPER BOUND ON THE GROUND STATE ENERGY</pattern>
    <template>For a Hamiltonian $\hat H$ bounded below, any normalized trial state $|\psi\rangle$ satisfies $\langle \psi|\hat H|\psi\rangle \ge E_0$, where $E_0$ is the ground-state energy. Minimizing the expectation value over a trial family yields the best upper bound within that family and often good approximations to $E_0$ and the ground-state wavefunction.</template>
  </category>
